    "q-52115": {
      "dom_id": "step-q52115",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a-b?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a=7 \\\\ 3a+6b=15\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the first equation is already solved for $a:$\n\n$$\na=7\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=7$ into $3a+6b=15,$ then we can find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+6b & =15 \\\\ 3(7)+6b & =15 \\\\ 6b & =-6 \\\\ b & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=7,\\,b=-1.$ So,\n\n$$\na-b=7-(-1)=7+1=8.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
